## Portainer's SELinux domain (SPEC.md §spec:portainer-service).
##
## Portainer holds the Docker control socket, which is root-equivalent access to
## the machine. Reaching that socket from inside a container needs an exemption
## from the machine's mandatory access control. This module is that exemption,
## and nothing more: the rest of the machine stays Enforcing.
##
## Two permissions are missing from the shipped policy and only two are added:
## writing the socket file, and connecting to the daemon behind it. Both come
## from container_stream_connect(), which container-selinux maintains for exactly
## this purpose - the rules are not written out here, so they stay correct as
## upstream changes them.
##
## Why a domain of its own rather than granting container_t: container_t is every
## container on the machine. Granting it there would hand the Docker socket to
## anything the operator ever deploys through Portainer, which is broader than
## running Portainer with full privileges - the thing §spec:portainer-service
## rejected. kantainer-portainer.service opts this one container in with
## --security-opt label=type:kantainer_portainer_t; nothing else can.

policy_module(kantainer_portainer, 1.0.0)

gen_require(`
	attribute container_domain;
	attribute container_file_type;
	type container_runtime_t;
')

# Portainer's persistent data under /var/lib/portainer, and the staged
# administrator password. Its own type, so no other container can read either.
type kantainer_portainer_file_t;
container_file(kantainer_portainer_file_t)

container_domain_template(kantainer_portainer, kantainer_portainer)

# Docker starts containers with NoNewPrivileges, which needs this transition.
container_runtime_typebounds(kantainer_portainer_t)

# The exemption itself.
container_stream_connect(kantainer_portainer_t)
